      { "text": "Aldosterone surge during recovery upregulates ENaC on sweat ducts and increases sweat-volume output.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Aldosterone-mediated ENaC upregulation is a slow acclimation response operating over days to weeks that REDUCES sweat salt content, not the cause of acute profuse defervescent sweating. This would be the correct answer if asking the basis of long-term heat acclimation." },
      { "text": "Loss of brown adipose tissue thermogenesis allows the resting metabolic rate to fall and the patient cools passively.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Adults have minimal contribution from brown adipose tissue to overall heat production, and the crisis is characterized by ACTIVE sweating and vasodilation, not passive cooling. This would be the correct answer if asking how a neonate stops generating heat as ambient temperature rises." },
      { "text": "Inhibition of post-ganglionic sympathetic cholinergic vasodilator fibers withdraws cutaneous arteriolar tone and warm blood pools in the skin.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Vasodilation during the fever crisis reflects ACTIVATION, not inhibition, of sympathetic cholinergic fibers to cutaneous arterioles; the pathway is engaged exactly as in any heat-dissipation response. This would be the correct answer if asking what happens during cold-induced cutaneous vasoconstriction." },
      { "text": "Set point drops back to normal; core temperature is now perceived as too high, triggering vasodilation and sweating.", "explanation": "Correct. The faculty described the crisis exactly as in this vignette: once the inciting pyrogen is removed (here by antibiotic clearance of bacteria), PGE2 synthesis stops and the hypothalamic set point abruptly drops to its normal value. The actual core temperature still being elevated is now perceived as ABOVE the new (normal) set point, so the hypothalamus engages the same effectors used in any heat stress: cutaneous vasodilation and profuse sweating. This is the mirror image of the chills phase, when an elevated set point above actual temperature triggered vasoconstriction and shivering." }
