      { "text": "Aldosterone-driven Na reabsorption in renal collecting ducts expands plasma volume and increases the heat capacity of blood enough to raise core temperature.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Aldosterone-driven Na reabsorption operates on a timescale of hours to days, not minutes, and changes in blood heat capacity from volume expansion cannot raise core temperature by 1.3 C in 30 minutes. This would be the correct answer if asking how chronic heat acclimation expands plasma volume." },
      { "text": "Sympathetic withdrawal from cutaneous arterioles produces such intense vasodilation that conducted heat from the environment overwhelms the cooling capacity.", "explanation": "Incorrect. The environment in the vignette is 22 C — cooler than skin — so the gradient still favors heat LOSS from the body to the environment, not gain; the heat storage during exercise comes from endogenous muscle metabolism, not from absorbed environmental heat. This would be the correct answer if the patient were exercising in a 38 C environment, where environmental gradient also favors heat gain." },
      { "text": "Activation of brown adipose tissue UCP1 by exercise-induced catecholamines accounts for most of the rise in core temperature.", "explanation": "Incorrect. BAT contribution in adults is minimal, and exercise-induced heat production is overwhelmingly from working SKELETAL muscle, not BAT. This would be the correct answer if asking how a neonate responds to acute cold exposure." },
      { "text": "Skeletal muscle becomes the dominant heat source (~90% of total) with metabolic rate rising 6 to 10-fold; warm muscle blood warms the core.", "explanation": "Correct. The faculty stated that during external work or exercise, skeletal muscle becomes the principal source of heat and may account for up to 90% of total heat production; metabolic rate can rise 6 to 8-fold in healthy young sedentary men and 10-fold or more in trained athletes. Heat generated locally in working muscle warms muscle blood by 1 to 2 C, and this warm blood returning to the core raises core temperature even though cutaneous vasodilation and sweating are simultaneously dissipating heat at a maximum sustainable rate. The lecturer emphasized that this small rise in core temperature is the stimulus that drives further activation of heat dissipation through the preoptic warmth-sensitive neurons." }
